    {
      "id": "event-handler-behavior-instructions",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Handler Contract Sketch",
      "root": false,
      "position": {
        "x": 380,
        "y": 1580
      },
      "width": 760,
      "height": 300,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 210
        },
        {
          "id": "guided-by",
          "key": "guided-by",
          "label": "guided by",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 180,
          "role": "relationship",
          "allowedEdgeTypes": [
            "reference"
          ],
          "metadata": {
            "relationship": true,
            "role": "relationship"
          }
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "event-handler-behavior"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Event handler contract (sketch)\n\nAn Event Handler is the reaction half of \"when this happens, do that.\" These are responsibilities, not a schema; any field names are illustrative.\n\n## Listens\n\n- One or more triggers, each a source and an event key, for example a Property's `property.changed`.\n- An enabled switch. A disabled handler stays on the graph and says so.\n\n## Decides\n\n- Optional conditions on the occurrence, checked before any code runs: only when the value changed, only for this state, only for this source.\n- Conditions are visible on the node. A handler with none runs for every matching occurrence.\n\n## Receives\n\n- The occurrence: its payload, its source, which trigger fired, its cause chain, and its identifiers.\n- A documented input, not ad hoc lookups.\n\n## Reacts\n\n- Inline code or a call to a Method.\n- Declares what it may change: nodes, Properties, or whole graphs. Declared effects are what make a dry run and a review meaningful.\n\n## Guards\n\n- Deterministic order among handlers of one event.\n- Loop protection across chained events, a time limit, and failure isolation so one handler cannot block another.\n\n## Reports\n\n- Last run: outcome, time, duration, and error.\n- Available on the node and to the graph, not only as a passing notification.\n\n## Test it\n\n- Fire the handler with a sample occurrence in dry-run mode and see what it would change.\n\nOnly a trigger and a reaction are required. The simple case stays one trigger and one reaction."
        },
        "interfaceContract": {
          "version": 1,
          "receivesViewContent": true
        }
      },
